 "findings": {
  "low_level": [
   "The pivot bin's members draw a ticket with atomicAdd with return on one constant shared address and store a (twiddled key, inverted index) pair to a 32-word buffer, warp 0 loads one pair per lane after a barrier and runs k_rem rounds: one redux.max per round on the packed word (key16 << 16 | inverted index) for bf16, two for fp32 (the key, then the inverted index among the lanes holding that key). The items above the pivot bin keep N6's per-item ticket scatter.",
   "Registers of the latency kernel: 46 at N 1024 and 80 at N 4096 under not_guaranteed (identical to B1_n6b), 58 and 128 under prefer_smaller_index against 58 and 128 for B1_n6b, so the finish adds no registers and no spill. Temp storage grows by 264 bytes (the pivot buffer and its counter).",
   "The constant-address pivot ticket is of the kind ptxas warp-aggregates (T23: S2R SR_LANEID and SR_LTMASK, VOTE, POPC, elected atomic, SHFL per site), one site per item slot; the second build N9_w2fin2 draws both tickets from one class-indexed counter as B1_idx does and is measured separately."
  ],
  "algorithmic": [
   "Against B1_n6b in the same lock section (B200, cycles per call, random normal input): under not_guaranteed the warp finish is slower in every cell, bf16 N 1024 k 16 1770 against 1688 (plus 5 percent), k 64 2095 against 2042, k 256 2359 against 2333, N 4096 k 16 2957 against 2749 (plus 8 percent), k 64 4107 against 4043, k 256 5082 against 4570 (plus 11 percent), fp32 N 1024 k 16 1800 against 1664 (plus 8 percent), N 4096 k 256 5010 against 4464 (plus 12 percent). Under prefer_smaller_index it is faster in every cell: bf16 N 1024 k 16 1989 against 2233 (minus 11 percent), N 4096 k 64 4403 against 6193 (minus 29 percent), k 256 5414 against 6913 (minus 22 percent), fp32 N 1024 k 16 2022 against 2094 (minus 3 percent), N 4096 k 64 4408 against 5096 (minus 14 percent), k 256 5361 against 5979 (minus 10 percent). Throughput follows the same signs (bf16 N 4096 k 64 prefer_smaller_index 413 against 298 Gelem per s).",
   "Why the not_guaranteed cells lose: N6b already ends after the first pass by count or by the tie class in most rows of the kill cell (the host simulation puts the first-pass exit at 0.97 of the bf16 N 1024 k 16 rows and 0.73 of the fp32 rows), so the second pass that N9 removes is rarely executed there, while the warp-finish path adds a ticket per item slot, a barrier, the buffer round trip and the rounds to every row it takes. Where the second pass does run often (fp32 N 4096 k 256, first-pass exit 0.14) the finish still costs more than the pass it replaces, which points at the aggregated constant-address tickets (one dependent S2R chain per item slot, 16 slots at N 4096) rather than at the rounds (k_rem is 1 to 7).",
   "Why the prefer_smaller_index cells win: the warp finish is exact on (key, index) by construction and replaces N6's warp-redundant rank (ballot table, barrier, shuffle scan over the slots) for the ties, which is the whole gap of 10 to 29 percent between N6b's two requirements at these cells."
  ],
  "negative": [
   "The N9 kill criterion fails: N9 does not take 15 percent off B1_n6b at N 1024 k 16 bf16 (1770 against 1688, threshold 1430) nor at N 4096 (2957 against 2749, threshold 2340) under not_guaranteed. The card's cost model charged N6 a full second pass per call and credited the finish with about 150 fixed cycles, both wrong by the measurement: the pass runs in a minority of rows and the finish costs more than 150 cycles with constant-address tickets.",
   "The two-redux fp32 round (key, then inverted index) is as fast as the one-redux bf16 round within the noise at these k_rem (1 to 7 rounds), so the round form is not what separates the two types here."
  ],
  "surprises": [
   "The same code is a 10 to 29 percent gain under prefer_smaller_index: at block scope the cheapest exact tie resolution for a small pivot set is a warp of redux rounds on (key, index), not a block-wide rank, which also holds for B4's finish.",
   "N6b's first-pass exit rate on bf16 logits is so high (the pivot bin is a single bf16 value in nearly every row from N 4096 on) that the second pass is already a rare path for bf16 at k at most 64."
  ],
  "open_questions": [
   "N9_w2fin2 (batch n9b, same session, one class-indexed ticket per item as in B1_idx) measured 1722 against 1686 at bf16 N 1024 k 16 and 1851 against 2041 at k 64, 3359 against 4043 at N 4096 k 64 under not_guaranteed, 3651 against 6188 at N 4096 k 64 under prefer_smaller_index: the aggregated constant-address ticket cost 50 to 750 cycles per call in this first build, see card N9_w2fin2_b200_20261025_1.",
   "A finish that skips the ticket entirely (the pivot members are at most 32, a ballot per item slot and a warp-level prefix would place them without atomics) was not built; T22 says per-slot ballots form a dependent chain, so the gain is unclear.",
   "Whether N6b itself should take the W2 finish only under prefer_smaller_index (where it replaces the rank) and keep the second pass under not_guaranteed."
  ]
 },
